  "semantic_claims": [
    {
      "claim_id": "majority-ground",
      "proposition": "Applying Twombly, Iqbal, Dudenhoeffer, and Hughes to ERISA's prudent-person text in 29 U.S.C. 1104(a)(1)(B) ('an enterprise of a like character and with like aims'), the Court holds that a complaint seeking to infer an imprudent fiduciary process from fund underperformance must plead a meaningful benchmark\u2014a comparator sharing similar investment aims and risk-return profile\u2014to rule out the obvious alternative explanation that the return differential resulted from different investment objectives rather than fiduciary imprudence."
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "ground-breadth",
      "proposition": "The Court announces a general pleading rule for ERISA underperformance claims across circuits while explicitly clarifying two limits: first, a 'meaningful benchmark' requires similar aims and risk profile rather than an identical twin or identical underlying asset allocation, and second, non-comparative or direct process/asset-allocation challenges need not plead a performance benchmark when not relying on underperformance, though Intel's non-performance allegations independently failed on their own terms."
    }
  ],
